{"name":"Maroon","hex":"#800000","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Maroon","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Alternative form of maroon (“descendant of escaped slaves”)."]},{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A surname."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Maroon"}],"entries":[{"title":"Maroon","blocks":[{"text":"Maroon is a brownish red color that takes its name from the French word marron, meaning chestnut. Marron is also one of the French translations for \"brown\"."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9d/Textile_LACMA_M.55.12.27.jpg/330px-Textile_LACMA_M.55.12.27.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maroon","wikidata":"Q25393814"},{"title":"Maroon (song)","blocks":[{"text":"\"Maroon\" is a song by the American singer-songwriter Taylor Swift from her tenth original studio album, Midnights (2022). Written and produced by Swift and Jack Antonoff, \"Maroon\" is a ballad that combines dream pop, synth-pop, electropop, and trip hop. Its ambient production consists of elongated trap beats, reverbed and layered vocals, and an oscillating electric guitar note. The lyrics are about the haunting memories of a past relationship in New York and associate them with different shades of red such as maroon, burgundy, and scarlet."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maroon_(song)","wikidata":"Q114804768"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Kiasma_Theatre_%281%29%2C_1998.jpg/500px-Kiasma_Theatre_%281%29%2C_1998.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Kiasma_Theatre_%281%29%2C_1998.jpg/500px-Kiasma_Theatre_%281%29%2C_1998.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AKiasma_Theatre_(1)%2C_1998.jpg"},{"s